<article class="help-article p-4">
    <header class="help-article-header mb-4">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined help-article-icon">swap_horiz</span>
        <h1 class="help-article-title">Mapping Models</h1>
        <p class="help-article-lead">
            A mapping model describes how data moves from a frozen model version to the version currently being edited when the change needs an explicit migration.
        </p>
    </header>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Create a mapping model</h2>
    <ol class="help-list">
        <li>Choose <strong>New Version</strong> in the model editor. Singularity freezes the current model and opens the next version for editing.</li>
        <li>Open <strong>Mapping Models</strong> from the editor menu.</li>
        <li>Choose a frozen source version. Singularity creates one inferred mapping model from that version to the current one.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        A frozen version can be the source of only one mapping model for the current destination. The generated name joins the source and destination version names with <code>To</code> and removes whitespace. It is shown in the inspector but cannot be edited.
    </p>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Entity maps</h2>
    <p>
        Each entity map pairs an optional source entity with an optional destination entity. Singularity infers the mapping type from the two sides and their version hashes.
    </p>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><strong>Add</strong> &mdash; the entity exists only in the destination.</li>
        <li><strong>Remove</strong> &mdash; the entity exists only in the source.</li>
        <li><strong>Copy</strong> &mdash; both entity versions have the same version hash.</li>
        <li><strong>Transform</strong> &mdash; the entity exists on both sides but its shape changed.</li>
        <li><strong>Custom</strong> &mdash; a subclass of <code>EntityMigrationPolicy</code> performs the migration. The project class must resolve through its autoloader.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="help-callout">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined">warning</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Custom maps require a migration policy.</strong>
            Singularity will not upgrade the store while any custom entity map has no valid policy class.
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Property maps</h2>
    <p>
        Attribute and relationship maps name a destination property and use a value expression to read from the source object. A destination attribute without a property map keeps its default value. The editor shows uncovered attributes before an upgrade.
    </p>
    <h2 class="help-heading">Upgrade</h2>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><strong>Upgrade Store</strong> writes the archived mapping model as a <code>.cdm</code> file in the generated project resources and hands the store back to its source model. Migration begins the next time the store opens.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The migration engine locates a mapping model by the source and destination entity version hashes stored inside the file. If the frozen source file is missing, or a custom entity map has no valid policy, the upgrade action remains unavailable.
    </p>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Remove a mapping model</h2>
    <p>
        Removing a mapping model always deletes its editable record from Singularity. The generated <code>.cdm</code> file remains on disk unless <strong>Delete mapping model files automatically</strong> is enabled in <a class="help-link" href="#preferences" data-help-anchor="preferences">Preferences</a>.
    </p>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li>The preference is disabled by default.</li>
        <li>When enabled, removing a mapping model also permanently removes its generated <code>.cdm</code> file if that file exists.</li>
        <li>Frozen source versions and other <code>.mom</code> files are never removed by this preference.</li>
        <li>Removing an entity map or property map does not remove a file; only a complete mapping model owns a <code>.cdm</code> file.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
